* ctrl + c to cancel a process

KILL to send signal to a process (kill -l)
PID (process ID - number that identifies process), signal number (-9, -15)
(to terminate a process and save changes, use signal term 15, to force a process to stop, use 9)
Eg. kill -15 8765

TOP - to see all processes running on your computer in real time - resource usage of sys.(updated by def. every 3secs)
TERMS
load average - how many processes are waiting for cpu(last 1 min, 5 & 15 mins)
tasks stored in system
when a child process is finished, it becomes a zombie (dead process)

CPU Breakdown
User cpu(us) - used by normal apps
System cpu (sy) - used by Linux
Idle cpu - unused
MiB Mem (RAM)
buff/cache - cached data(temporary) kept in RAM
MiB Swap - hard drive(emergency memory when RAM fills up)
user - who started process
PR - priority
NI -niceness (- aggressive priority, + lower priority)
VIRT - Virtual mem(total memory processes can access)
RES - Resident mem (physical RAM currently used)
SHR - shared mem (mem shared with other processes)
S -status eg. running, sleeping, zombie
%CPU - how much CPU processes use
%MEM -how much RAM processes use
Time - total cpu time used since process started
Command - process name 


* to sort process by CPU usage use shift + p and shift + m to sort by mem usage
* if a process is taking too many resources or frozen, press k to kill
* htop is an app that does same as top command

* to pause a process running in the terminal eg. firefox use ctrl z
to resume, fuse fg(foreground) or bg (background)

